  "generator": "tests/story_bench.c (make story)",
  "policy": "A pilot who reads the instruments on screen, not the code: it glances at the glide-needed gauge (distance to the middle of the strip over height) every 0.2-0.6 s. Above 23-28 it runs the motor at the best-glide angle until the gauge reads 2-7 lower or the battery is flat (with no motor it stretches the glide); below 13-21 it lowers the nose to 0-2.5 deg to lose height, and over the strip it keeps the nose down; otherwise it holds a cruise angle of 3-7 deg. Below 3-12 m it flares to 6-12 deg. Its hand comes from the run's own stirred seed, re-drawn for every hop and every relaunch. Choices follow the path field.",
  "crisis": "the LOW gauge red (glide needed steeper than 1:25) at any moment in the hop, or an outlanding (an outlanding counts as a crisis)",
  "failure": "an outlanding: the Kestrel touched down short of the strip, past its end or too hard (sink 2 m/s or more, path steeper than 0.15 rad), or was still in the air after 360 s; a farmer's trailer brings her back and the hop is flown again",
  "segments": ["Upper Field to Weir Strip", "to Orchard (quarry or ridge)", "Orchard to Mill Strip", "Mill to Ferry Point, in the valley wind", "to the breaker's yard on the coast"],
  "note": "16 seeds x 12 runs; every run has its own stirred seed; the path comes from the run number.",
